# HTML reporting guidelines

Every HTML report plan-anchor produces follows this page. The reports are all rendered by `scripts/change_report.py`:

| Report | Command | Kind |
|---|---|---|
| Change report (`references/change-report.md`) | `change_report.py --anchors --baseline-rev ...` | `change` |
| Trace report (`references/record-trace.md`) | `change_report.py --trace <trace.json>` | `trace` |
| Record-cleanup report (`references/record-cleanup.md`) | `change_report.py --trace <trace.json> --proposals <proposals.json>` | `cleanup` |

## Where a report goes

Reports are saved in the repository's website folder, `reports_dir` in `.sc/plan-anchor/settings.yaml`, relative to the folder holding `.sc/`. When it is unset the folder is `site/reports/plan-anchor`. Set it with `python3 "<this skill's directory>/scripts/plan_anchor_settings.py" set reports_dir <folder>` and commit the settings file. `change_report.py` writes there when `--out` is not given. Pass `--out` only when the owner names another folder.

## Sources saved beside it

A report can always be rebuilt from what is saved with it. `change_report.py` copies its sources to `<reports_dir>/<name>/sources/`:

- change report: `anchors.json` now and at the baseline revision, the run files it read, the drift review, the fixture checks and the tuning results;
- trace and record-cleanup reports: the trace JSON, which holds every record as Raptor served it and the merged annotations, and the proposals file, which holds the owner's approvals.

`data.sources` lists them. Commit the page, its `<name>/` folder and the sidecar together.

## Names

A report's name is `YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-<kind>-<subject>`, the UTC time it was generated, its kind (`change`, `trace` or `cleanup`) and its subject (the plan folder's name). Two reports never share a name: when one already exists, `-2`, `-3` and so on are appended. `change_report.py` uses this name when `--name` is not given. Pass `--name` only to rebuild a report under its existing name.

## Index

After writing a report, add it to `<reports_dir>/index.html`: create the file when it is missing, then add `data.index_entry` (one `<li>` linking the page) as the first item of its list, newest first. Never change or remove another entry. If the repository builds its reports index at deploy from the stamped file names, that build replaces this step; do not edit a generated index.

## Shared report language

Every report says these things one way:

- **Header**: the title, then a subtitle naming who computed what, then `Generated: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM UTC`.
- **Jev**: numbers Jev answered are shown as `Jev P(yes)`; classes and verdicts the scripts compute from them say "computed by jev_judge.py from Jev's P(yes)". Jev writes no prose in a report.
- **Agent text**: anything an agent wrote is labelled "written by the anchor-cleanup-proposer agent, not Jev" (or that agent's name), next to the text.
- **Owner**: the owner's verbatim words are "Owner said: “...”"; the owner's reply to proposals is "Owner reply"; a decision on one panel is "Owner decision".
- **Buttons**: `{}` "Copy JSON" copies the panel's data; ⎘ "Copy Context" copies the panel as plain text.
- **Reply line**: "Reply e.g.: approve 1-5, reject 6, change 7 to: <text>".
- **Legend**: trace cells are coloured `serves`, `related`, `exceeds`, `conflicts`, `none`; a change report's status is `PASS`, `DRIFT` or `FAIL`.
- **Footer**: "Generated by plan-anchoring change_report.py", then where the sidecar and the saved sources are.

Showing the owner the report is part of every workflow that produces one: send the page's path or link, and say where its sources are.
